#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:2
msgid "Processes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:4
msgid ""
"libuv offers considerable child process management, abstracting the "
"platform differences and allowing communication with the child process "
"using streams or named pipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:8
msgid ""
"A common idiom in Unix is for every process to do one thing and do it "
"well. In such a case, a process often uses multiple child processes to "
"achieve tasks (similar to using pipes in shells). A multi-process model "
"with messages may also be easier to reason about compared to one with "
"threads and shared memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:14
msgid ""
"A common refrain against event-based programs is that they cannot take "
"advantage of multiple cores in modern computers. In a multi-threaded "
"program the kernel can perform scheduling and assign different threads to"
" different cores, improving performance. But an event loop has only one "
"thread.  The workaround can be to launch multiple processes instead, with"
" each process running an event loop, and each process getting assigned to"
" a separate CPU core."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:23
msgid "Spawning child processes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:25
msgid ""
"The simplest case is when you simply want to launch a process and know "
"when it exits. This is achieved using ``uv_spawn``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:28 ../../src/guide/processes.rst:58
msgid "spawn/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:37
msgid ""
"``options`` is implicitly initialized with zeros since it is a global "
"variable.  If you change ``options`` to a local variable, remember to "
"initialize it to null out all unused fields::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:43
msgid ""
"The ``uv_process_t`` struct only acts as the handle, all options are set "
"via ``uv_process_options_t``. To simply launch a process, you need to set"
" only the ``file`` and ``args`` fields. ``file`` is the program to "
"execute. Since ``uv_spawn`` uses :man:`execvp(3)` internally, there is no"
" need to supply the full path. Finally as per underlying conventions, "
"**the arguments array has to be one larger than the number of arguments, "
"with the last element being NULL**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:50
msgid ""
"After the call to ``uv_spawn``, ``uv_process_t.pid`` will contain the "
"process ID of the child process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:53
msgid ""
"The exit callback will be invoked with the *exit status* and the type of "
"*signal* which caused the exit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:56
msgid ""
"Note that it is important **not** to call ``uv_close`` before the exit "
"callback."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:65
msgid "It is **required** to close the process watcher after the process exits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:68
msgid "Changing process parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:70
msgid ""
"Before the child process is launched you can control the execution "
"environment using fields in ``uv_process_options_t``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:74
msgid "Change execution directory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:76
msgid "Set ``uv_process_options_t.cwd`` to the corresponding directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:79
msgid "Set environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:81
msgid ""
"``uv_process_options_t.env`` is a null-terminated array of strings, each "
"of the form ``VAR=VALUE`` used to set up the environment variables for "
"the process. Set this to ``NULL`` to inherit the environment from the "
"parent (this) process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:86
msgid "Option flags"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:88
msgid ""
"Setting ``uv_process_options_t.flags`` to a bitwise OR of the following "
"flags, modifies the child process behaviour:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:91
msgid ""
"``UV_PROCESS_SETUID`` - sets the child's execution user ID to "
"``uv_process_options_t.uid``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:92
msgid ""
"``UV_PROCESS_SETGID`` - sets the child's execution group ID to "
"``uv_process_options_t.gid``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:94
msgid ""
"Changing the UID/GID is only supported on Unix, ``uv_spawn`` will fail on"
" Windows with ``UV_ENOTSUP``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:97
msgid ""
"``UV_PROCESS_WINDOWS_VERBATIM_ARGUMENTS`` - No quoting or escaping of "
"``uv_process_options_t.args`` is done on Windows. Ignored on Unix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:99
msgid ""
"``UV_PROCESS_DETACHED`` - Starts the child process in a new session, "
"which will keep running after the parent process exits. See example "
"below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:103
msgid "Detaching processes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:105
msgid ""
"Passing the flag ``UV_PROCESS_DETACHED`` can be used to launch daemons, "
"or child processes which are independent of the parent so that the parent"
" exiting does not affect it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:109
msgid "detach/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:116
msgid ""
"Just remember that the handle is still monitoring the child, so your "
"program won't exit. Use ``uv_unref()`` if you want to be more *fire-and-"
"forget*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:120
msgid "Sending signals to processes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:122
msgid ""
"libuv wraps the standard ``kill(2)`` system call on Unix and implements "
"one with similar semantics on Windows, with *one caveat*: all of "
"``SIGTERM``, ``SIGINT`` and ``SIGKILL``, lead to termination of the "
"process. The signature of ``uv_kill`` is::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:129
msgid ""
"For processes started using libuv, you may use ``uv_process_kill`` "
"instead, which accepts the ``uv_process_t`` watcher as the first "
"argument, rather than the pid. In this case, **remember to call** "
"``uv_close`` on the watcher _after_ the exit callback has been called."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:135
msgid "Signals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:137
msgid ""
"libuv provides wrappers around Unix signals with `some Windows support "
"<https://docs.libuv.org/en/v1.x/signal.html#signal>`_ as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:140
msgid ""
"Use ``uv_signal_init()`` to initialize a handle and associate it with a "
"loop. To listen for particular signals on that handler, use "
"``uv_signal_start()`` with the handler function. Each handler can only be"
" associated with one signal number, with subsequent calls to "
"``uv_signal_start()`` overwriting earlier associations. Use "
"``uv_signal_stop()`` to stop watching. Here is a small example "
"demonstrating the various possibilities:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:147
msgid "signal/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:155
msgid ""
"``uv_run(loop, UV_RUN_NOWAIT)`` is similar to ``uv_run(loop, "
"UV_RUN_ONCE)`` in that it will process only one event. UV_RUN_ONCE blocks"
" if there are no pending events, while UV_RUN_NOWAIT will return "
"immediately. We use NOWAIT so that one of the loops isn't starved because"
" the other one has no pending activity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:161
msgid ""
"Send ``SIGUSR1`` to the process, and you'll find the handler being "
"invoked 4 times, one for each ``uv_signal_t``. The handler just stops "
"each handle, so that the program exits. This sort of dispatch to all "
"handlers is very useful. A server using multiple event loops could ensure"
" that all data was safely saved before termination, simply by every loop "
"adding a watcher for ``SIGINT``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:169
msgid "Child Process I/O"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:171
msgid ""
"A normal, newly spawned process has its own set of file descriptors, with"
" 0, 1 and 2 being ``stdin``, ``stdout`` and ``stderr`` respectively. "
"Sometimes you may want to share file descriptors with the child. For "
"example, perhaps your applications launches a sub-command and you want "
"any errors to go in the log file, but ignore ``stdout``. For this you'd "
"like to have ``stderr`` of the child be the same as the stderr of the "
"parent. In this case, libuv supports *inheriting* file descriptors. In "
"this sample, we invoke the test program, which is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:180
msgid "proc-streams/test.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:184
msgid ""
"The actual program ``proc-streams`` runs this while sharing only "
"``stderr``. The file descriptors of the child process are set using the "
"``stdio`` field in ``uv_process_options_t``. First set the "
"``stdio_count`` field to the number of file descriptors being set. "
"``uv_process_options_t.stdio`` is an array of ``uv_stdio_container_t``, "
"which is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:201
msgid ""
"where flags can have several values. Use ``UV_IGNORE`` if it isn't going "
"to be used. If the first three ``stdio`` fields are marked as "
"``UV_IGNORE`` they'll redirect to ``/dev/null``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:205
msgid ""
"Since we want to pass on an existing descriptor, we'll use "
"``UV_INHERIT_FD``. Then we set the ``fd`` to ``stderr``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:208
msgid "proc-streams/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:215
msgid ""
"If you run ``proc-stream`` you'll see that only the line \"This is "
"stderr\" will be displayed. Try marking ``stdout`` as being inherited and"
" see the output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:218
msgid ""
"It is dead simple to apply this redirection to streams.  By setting "
"``flags`` to ``UV_INHERIT_STREAM`` and setting ``data.stream`` to the "
"stream in the parent process, the child process can treat that stream as "
"standard I/O. This can be used to implement something like CGI_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:225
msgid "A sample CGI script/executable is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:227
msgid "cgi/tick.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:231
msgid ""
"The CGI server combines the concepts from this chapter and "
":doc:`networking` so that every client is sent ten ticks after which that"
" connection is closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:234 ../../src/guide/processes.rst:244
msgid "cgi/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:241
msgid ""
"Here we simply accept the TCP connection and pass on the socket "
"(*stream*) to ``invoke_cgi_script``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:251
msgid ""
"The ``stdout`` of the CGI script is set to the socket so that whatever "
"our tick script prints, gets sent to the client. By using processes, we "
"can offload the read/write buffering to the operating system, so in terms"
" of convenience this is great. Just be warned that creating processes is "
"a costly task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:259
msgid "Parent-child IPC"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:261
msgid ""
"A parent and child can have one or two way communication over a pipe "
"created by settings ``uv_stdio_container_t.flags`` to a bit-wise "
"combination of ``UV_CREATE_PIPE`` and ``UV_READABLE_PIPE`` or "
"``UV_WRITABLE_PIPE``. The read/write flag is from the perspective of the "
"child process.  In this case, the ``uv_stream_t* stream`` field must be "
"set to point to an initialized, unopened ``uv_pipe_t`` instance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:269
msgid "New stdio Pipes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:271
msgid ""
"The ``uv_pipe_t`` structure represents more than just `pipe(7)`_ (or "
"``|``), but supports any streaming file-like objects. On Windows, the "
"only object of that description is the `Named Pipe`_.  On Unix, this "
"could be any of `Unix Domain Socket`_, or derived from `mkfifo(1)`_, or "
"it could actually be a `pipe(7)`_.  When ``uv_spawn`` initializes a "
"``uv_pipe_t`` due to the `UV_CREATE_PIPE` flag, it opts for creating a "
"`socketpair(2)`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:278
msgid ""
"This is intended for the purpose of allowing multiple libuv processes to "
"communicate with IPC. This is discussed below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:289
msgid "Arbitrary process IPC"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:291
msgid ""
"Since domain sockets [#]_ can have a well known name and a location in "
"the file-system they can be used for IPC between unrelated processes. The"
" D-BUS_ system used by open source desktop environments uses domain "
"sockets for event notification. Various applications can then react when "
"a contact comes online or new hardware is detected. The MySQL server also"
" runs a domain socket on which clients can interact with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:300
msgid ""
"When using domain sockets, a client-server pattern is usually followed "
"with the creator/owner of the socket acting as the server. After the "
"initial setup, messaging is no different from TCP, so we'll re-use the "
"echo server example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:304
msgid "pipe-echo-server/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:311
msgid ""
"We name the socket ``echo.sock`` which means it will be created in the "
"local directory. This socket now behaves no different from TCP sockets as"
" far as the stream API is concerned. You can test this server using "
"`socat`_::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:317
msgid "A client which wants to connect to a domain socket will use::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:321
msgid ""
"where ``name`` will be ``echo.sock`` or similar. On Unix systems, "
"``name`` must point to a valid file (e.g. ``/tmp/echo.sock``). On "
"Windows, ``name`` follows a ``\\\\?\\pipe\\echo.sock`` format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:328
msgid "Sending file descriptors over pipes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:330
msgid ""
"The cool thing about domain sockets is that file descriptors can be "
"exchanged between processes by sending them over a domain socket. This "
"allows processes to hand off their I/O to other processes. Applications "
"include load-balancing servers, worker processes and other ways to make "
"optimum use of CPU. libuv only supports sending **TCP sockets or other "
"pipes** over pipes for now."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:336
msgid ""
"To demonstrate, we will look at an echo server implementation that hands "
"off clients to worker processes in a round-robin fashion. This program is"
" a bit involved, and while only snippets are included in the book, it is "
"recommended to read the full code to really understand it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:341
msgid ""
"The worker process is quite simple, since the file-descriptor is handed "
"over to it by the master."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:344 ../../src/guide/processes.rst:358
msgid "multi-echo-server/worker.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:351
msgid ""
"``queue`` is the pipe connected to the master process on the other end, "
"along which new file descriptors get sent. It is important to set the "
"``ipc`` argument of ``uv_pipe_init`` to 1 to indicate this pipe will be "
"used for inter-process communication! Since the master will write the "
"file handle to the standard input of the worker, we connect the pipe to "
"``stdin`` using ``uv_pipe_open``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:365
msgid ""
"First we call ``uv_pipe_pending_count()`` to ensure that a handle is "
"available to read out. If your program could deal with different types of"
" handles, ``uv_pipe_pending_type()`` can be used to determine the type. "
"Although ``accept`` seems odd in this code, it actually makes sense. What"
" ``accept`` traditionally does is get a file descriptor (the client) from"
" another file descriptor (The listening socket). Which is exactly what we"
" do here. Fetch the file descriptor (``client``) from ``queue``. From "
"this point the worker does standard echo server stuff."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:374
msgid ""
"Turning now to the master, let's take a look at how the workers are "
"launched to allow load balancing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:377 ../../src/guide/processes.rst:386
#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:405
msgid "multi-echo-server/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:383
msgid ""
"The ``child_worker`` structure wraps the process, and the pipe between "
"the master and the individual process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:393
msgid ""
"In setting up the workers, we use the nifty libuv function "
"``uv_cpu_info`` to get the number of CPUs so we can launch an equal "
"number of workers. Again it is important to initialize the pipe acting as"
" the IPC channel with the third argument as 1. We then indicate that the "
"child process' ``stdin`` is to be a readable pipe (from the point of view"
" of the child). Everything is straightforward till here. The workers are "
"launched and waiting for file descriptors to be written to their standard"
" input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:401
msgid ""
"It is in ``on_new_connection`` (the TCP infrastructure is initialized in "
"``main()``), that we accept the client socket and pass it along to the "
"next worker in the round-robin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:412
msgid ""
"The ``uv_write2`` call handles all the abstraction and it is simply a "
"matter of passing in the handle (``client``) as the right argument. With "
"this our multi-process echo server is operational."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:416
msgid ""
"Thanks to Kyle for `pointing out`_ that ``uv_write2()`` requires a non-"
"empty buffer even when sending handles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/processes.rst:423
msgid ""
"In this section domain sockets stands in for named pipes on Windows as "
"well."
msgstr ""

